export const t = {
  "1:א": `Whether by hand — see in Mishna Berurah that by hand one is exempt and forbidden, and this is according to the Sages against R' Eliezer; and see in Ran who wrote that according to R' Chananel the halacha follows R' Eliezer, since they explain the reason of the Sages and I did not understand his explanation, for R' Eliezer's first statement is specifically according to the Sages; and furthermore what is the language "they explain its reason" that he says; and furthermore that Rabeinu Asher in chapter Kol Gadol siman 16 proves explicitly that R' Chananel holds that plucking wool by hand is exempt like the Sages against R' Eliezer; but now that we merited to have R' Chananel's explanation we found explicitly that R' Chananel's words refer to R' Eliezer's second case of a large woman and a dwarf, where several Sages explain the reason why he is liable; and in the first case of R' Eliezer R' Chananel rules explicitly like the Sages — see there; and likewise on daf 74b — see there; and it appears Ran did not have the R' Chananel explanation we have, only some abbreviation mistakenly copied to him:`,
  "1:ב": `And liable, etc. — behold Tosafos wrote that this is specifically according to R' Yehuda, for melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah one is liable; but according to R' Shimon exempt even with a utensil; and because of this Acharonim wondered at Rosh and Tur who copied this law — behold they ruled like R' Shimon; and also Mechaber — how did he rule anonymously here, and in siman 316 seif 8 he sided with the first view to exempt, and Magen Avraham in s.k. 1 wrote that according to one who holds melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah exempt — it deals when he needs the hair; but he challenged his words, for if so why did he rule that in picking white from among black even for one he is liable — for nevertheless he does not need the hair itself, for even if we say it deals when he needs the hair — that one alone has no measure for liability; and because of this challenge one found someone who challenged thus; and it appears from this challenge Gra moved from Magen Avraham's words and cited on the law of picking black, etc., anonymously like Rambam's view that the halacha follows R' Yehuda in melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah — end; and likewise in Makos 20b he cited their words in name of Rashbam also on this statement of picking, etc. (and perhaps one can say with difficulty Magen Avraham's words, since once shearing is considered melacha even in one hair because through this he beautifies — automatically it is considered needed for its purpose also in what this hair alone needs); and also according to Magen Avraham's words it would be forced to explain that which is above in siman 328 s.k. 31 regarding hanging skin-tags and nails — see there also as needed; and likewise in responsum Chacham Tzvi siman 82 he rejected with two hands because of this challenge. And behold Gra wrote that Shulchan Aruch ruled anonymously here like Rambam's view in melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah that one is liable; but what shall we do with the challenge of all Acharonim that Shulchan Aruch contradicts itself, as above; and behold Rivash in siman 394 [and it was brought above in siman 303 and also in this siman] wrote that the melacha of shearing is considered melacha for all, even when he does not need the hair, for in the Mishkan they sheared tachash skins and not for the wool and hair but for the hide, such as tachash skins — and therefore one is liable whenever it is for its purpose even though he does not need the hair; and melacha needed for its purpose is, end; and similarly Rivash wrote almost the same in Ran's chiddushim on Shabbos: dispute by hand — Rashi [reason] on account of gozez, for even though he does not need the shearing, since he needs to beautify himself with this shearing — even in this he is liable for gozez, similar to shearing the shalach, where he is liable for gozez even where he does not need shearing but that the hide remain without hair so it be fit for tanning, end; and it is possible to say this is Shulchan Aruch's view and not like Tosafos; and I found in Chacham Tzvi that he also agreed that Shulchan Aruch's view is like Rivash and not like Tosafos; and behold I saw in Sefer Seder Taharah who wondered at Chacham Tzvi for agreeing to Rivash's view and wrote — behold Rosh wrote explicitly in Bechoros 25 and also brought there in name of Ramban that plucking wool from the animal before slaughter and likewise feathers from the bird is melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah — and therefore they hold also like Tosafos; and in truth it is no proof at all, as he himself rejected afterward, for it is different there, for that plucking is not for the hide but for slaughter; but where it is for the hide, even though he does not need shearing — they hold he is liable, as is written regarding gozez from the shalach; and likewise in our matter, since he takes nails or hair to beautify himself — this is called for his purpose, as above in name of Ran. And now that we clarified that this sugya in Shabbos 94b deals when he does not need the hair and nails — we are forced to say according to many early great ones who also hold like Rivash and Ran above — behold they are R' Chananel, Ramban, Semag, Rosh; and we explain them one by one. R' Chananel — he himself ruled like R' Shimon, and also ruled explicitly in our matter that with a utensil one is liable to a chatat; and also Ramban — he himself brought in chapter Kol Kitvei and proved that Rif rules like R' Shimon, and here Rif himself copied the entire sugya of hanging skin-tags and nails; and also Rosh and Semag — they explicitly agreed the halacha follows R' Shimon that melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah is exempt, and nevertheless both copied the entire sugya of hanging skin-tags and nails and picking white from among black that there is liability to a chatat — and therefore we must say all these poskim hold like Ran and Rivash above that this is called for its purpose and therefore Shulchan Aruch ruled anonymously thus. And behold I saw that Gra in this siman wrote from this sugya proof for Rambam's ruling that melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah one is liable; but according to all the aforementioned geonim who hold melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah exempt and nevertheless all copied the sugya — therefore they hold like Ran and Rivash above; and see in siman 316 end of seif 18 in Beur HaGra where he sided there the halacha is melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah exempt — and therefore he shows favor to every view that has a great source in the Talmud. And behold regarding a woman who forgot to cut nails from erev Shabbat and her immersion night falls on Shabbat — certainly l'chatchila proper that she beware to tell a non-Jew to cut with the hand and not with a utensil, for it is complete melacha according to Rambam; but when impossible by hand Magen Avraham sided to be lenient through a non-Jew even with a utensil, like Nekudot HaKesef in Yoreh De'ah siman 198, as we wrote above that here it is called melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah, for her intent is not adornment but for immersion; and it appears regarding toenails certainly better to permit through scraping than through a non-Jew; see in Perishah there in name of Maharar Daniel:`,
  "1:ג": `For two hairs — see in Mishna Berurah, and it is from Magen Avraham; and likewise is the agreement of all Acharonim for prohibition, and not like Eliyah Rabbah; and likewise Rambam explicitly in chapter 9 law 23 that all melachot of Shabbat — if one did half the measure there is prohibition; and automatically included is writing one letter or removing one hair and the like. And regarding whether it is d'oraisa or rabbinically — in Magen Avraham it appears it is d'oraisa like every half-measure of forbidden foods, for we rule like R' Yochanan that it is forbidden from the Torah, for the reason is it is fit to combine, as stated in Yoma daf 74 regarding shearing; and likewise if we say because it is written "all chelev" — reasonable we derive from it also for the entire Torah, as Tosafos wrote there that R' Yehuda holds because it is fit to combine therefore the Torah forbade; and if so the same in anything fit to combine [and Chacham Tzvi's words in siman 86 who wanted to distinguish with one answer between equals and wanted to prove his view from Tosafos are very strained]; and likewise is Rashi explicitly daf 74 s.v. vekhi and in Hagahot chapter 23 siman 11; and likewise Or Zarua in Halakhot Gedolot letter 59 and in Meiri's chiddushim daf 74 and Rabbeinu Yerucham part 2 chapter 14; and do not challenge our words from what they said daf 3: all exempt matters of Shabbat — exempt but forbidden, and Rashi explained forbidden rabbinically; and behold on daf 102b it states if they are not read together exempt, like one letter and similarly several mishnayot; and according to our words above it is Torah prohibition, and only exempt from chatat — one can say what Rashi wrote forbidden rabbinically intended to include all exempt matters that in any case have rabbinic prohibition; and the same here that there is Torah prohibition; and with this Nachalat Tzvi's words on writing are somewhat challenged, for he contradicts what he wrote at the beginning of the section; and perhaps what he wrote in all these is not precise and for practical law one should not move at all from all the aforementioned poskim's words:`,
  "1:ד": `And picks white from among black — study is required whether specifically when there was only one hair where his picking helps beautify — therefore considered melacha; or perhaps nevertheless he beautifies somewhat; and behold in Yerushalmi chapter Kol Gadol regarding borer it states: R' Yudan said — he sat on the pile and selected all day — not liable; and the commentator there explained because he accomplished nothing, for stones still remain [and see above what we wrote in siman 319 on this]; and at first glance the same in our matter according to Yerushalmi; and likewise implied somewhat in Makos 20b in the commentator there s.v. bamelaket — see there; and see in Tur Yoreh De'ah siman 182 in Beit Yosef there who sided that even in one white hair alone one transgresses on account of "a man shall not wear," etc.; and if so at first glance the same for Shabbat liability, for the baraita equates them:`,
  "1:ה": `Even for one he is liable — Kolbo wrote that in this even by hand one is liable; and see in Eshel Avraham the reason that the manner on a weekday is to pick by hand; but the Gemara challenge remains; and in my humble opinion it appears what they said "and the Sages agree with R' Eliezer" means even by hand and even for one; and Rif's version proves this — see there:`,
  "2:_": `A wart from his body — see what is written in Mishna Berurah; and likewise Taz in siman 336 at its end: one should beware not to pluck from his skin by hand or in other places, for it is like attached to the ground, as is implied at the beginning of Sanhedrin that man is like ground, end; and behold its law is true, as is explained in siman 328 s.k. 31 — see there; and even though there it deals with hanging skin-tags of nails according to Rashi — plainly the same in other places when they separated somewhat from the skin; and in Rambam chapter 9 it states explicitly hanging skin-tags of skin that separated; but what Taz hung the reason on account of man is like ground implies the reason is uprooting something from its growth — in Rashi Eruvin 103 s.v. im bekli it appears because of gozez; and likewise implied in Rambam chapter 9 that he counts all this in the melacha of gozez; and likewise Magen Avraham in siman 328 s.k. 35; and this is when done with a utensil, for this is the manner of shearing; by hand exempt but forbidden; and one can obligate them on account of uprooting something from its growth — end; for we already conclude in Bechoros 25 it is called back of the hand, for it is not his manner to uproot by hand but with a utensil; and when he does with a utensil, which is his manner — automatically his liability is for gozez, as stated there regarding plucking a wing from a bird liable for gozez. And know further that according to the conclusion there in Bechoros 25, in a matter whose manner is to pluck by hand one is liable for plucking for gozez — if so when there are some hanging skin-tags on his lips that did not mostly separate — perhaps one who plucks them is liable to a chatat even by hand, for the manner is to pluck there by hand and not with a utensil; and accordingly even when they mostly separated — permitted above in siman 328 to pluck by hand when they pain him — here forbidden, for here since it is his manner it is like a utensil:`,
  "3:א": `One who erases ink, etc. — behold the primary av melacha of erasing is when he erases two letters in order to write two other letters in their place, for all melachot of Shabbat we derive from the Mishkan; and in the Mishkan regarding this matter they sometimes wrote erroneously on the boards that they wrote on them as a sign in order to match them, and erased in order to write others in their place [and even though in the Mishkan sometimes an error fell in one letter — Chazal hold erasing is not considered from writing and we require specifically two letters also]; nevertheless we derive from this that likewise erasing ink or another smudge like this in order to write two letters in this place — liable, for the letter first written in error is as if it did not exist; and the primary liability of erasing is because he intended to write in this place; therefore liable even for erasing an ink smudge [Rosh, brought in Beit Yosef]. Peri Megadim wrote: just as we say in the Gemara that if he corrected one letter to complete the book he is liable because there one letter is considered significant — the same if he erased a corrupted letter in order to write another in its place and thereby the book is completed — liable for erasing — see there; and if so according to what we explain below from Rashi and Ri'az that even if he corrected one letter in the middle of the book he is liable for writing — automatically the same when he erases in this manner in order to write another in its place properly — liable for erasing; and if so what Mechaber wrote that we require in the place of erasing ink room to write two letters specifically — it deals when ink fell not in the place of letters, and therefore we require the place be fit to write two letters; but if ink fell on some letter and was corrupted and he erases in order to write another in its place properly — liable for erasing on this letter alone. And since we deal with laws of erasing I saw fit to copy what Peri Megadim sided on this: if in the erasing alone there was some fixing — such as something was written there that he was obligated to his fellow or his fellow to him and the like, and now we need to erase it — liable for the erasing alone even though not in order to write; and specifically in erasing a smudge and the like we require intent in order to write two letters; but here where the erasing itself is fixing — end. And I saw fit to bring several proofs for his words: just as it states in the mishnah regarding tearing in order to sew, for so it was in the Mishkan as stated in the Gemara — nevertheless it is known that even not in order to sew, whenever there is fixing in the tearing he is liable, for it is like in order to sew; and as we clarified at the end of the siman — the same in erasing, even though the mishnah states erasing in order to write and the reason is because so it was in the Mishkan that they erased some letter they erred in in order to write another in its place — nevertheless where in the erasing itself there is fixing it appears like in order to write; and there is proof from Tosefta chapter 12 law 7: you have none liable except one who erases in order to write, and one who erases in order to fix anything — liable; and see Rambam chapter 10 law 15 that wrote in demolishing we require in order to build, but if he demolished by way of spoiling — exempt — implies if not by way of spoiling but for some need it is like in order to build, since it was for some fixing it emerges from spoiling; and the same in our matter regarding erasing, for Rambam includes demolishing and erasing in one category, as is proven in chapter 1 law 18 — see there:`,
  "3:ב": `That is on parchment — see in Mishna Berurah where I copied Beit Yosef's practical law and his support; and even though in responsum Shevut Yaakov part 2 siman 4 he disagrees that removing wax from parchment is not included in erasing but in smearing when he spreads it by hand or with fingernails on parchment and paper [and a ledger is different, for their custom was to coat pages with wax in order to inscribe on the wax, as Rashi explained; and therefore when more wax dripped on the page the pages stick together; and therefore when he erases the upper wax in order to have place to write on it — included in erasing in order to write; unlike when wax drips on parchment or paper it does not stick so much and the writing is inside like placed in a box; and proof for this is in tefillin it is not invalidated because of lack of order when he removed the wax, as above in siman 332 s.k. 23 in Magen Avraham — therefore when he scrapes and removes wax it is not erasing but removing cover from above] — therefore he wrote that if he wants to remove wax from parchment in a manner that he not touch the wax by hand or fingernails, such as when the wax dried and he can remove it back-handed so the wax falls by itself — permitted to do so; nevertheless in practice one should not rule leniently against Bach and all the many great ones who hold like him; and Bach's view and all the above is since in any case the writing was smudged by the wax and through removing it the writing is revealed — included in erasing in order to write; and even though regarding tefillin we hold that through dripping wax above the letters beneath the wax are not nullified so as to later invalidate for lack of order — nevertheless regarding Shabbat, since initially they could not read it and now when he removes the wax he fixes the letters so they can read — included in erasing in order to fix, as written above; again I found one who also challenged Bach's words from this law of tefillin, as above; and it appears to me to answer as I wrote that nevertheless it is included in erasing in order to fix. And see in Chiddushei R' Akiva Eiger on siman 332 that Shevut Yaakov's view is in essence there is no erasing regarding wax, and nevertheless he agreed to the practical law also with all the great ones that one should not remove wax from letters of a book on Shabbat on account of fixing a utensil by fixing the book — see there. And Peri Megadim's view in Eshel Avraham appears he also holds like Bach that in wax on parchment and paper there is erasing and not like Shevut Yaakov — they did not mention except that he sided that specifically not in the place of letters then liable for erasing when intent is in order to write in that place; but in the place of letters there is no erasing and the lower writing automatically exists — only prohibition of moving wax exists — see there; nevertheless he did not fill his heart to rule thus in practice, for he wrote regarding scraping wax from two letters through a non-Jew — requires study; and one letter he permitted scraping through a non-Jew; and in Sefer Birkei Yosef it also appears he sided with Bach's practical law. And know that according to Bach and supporters the same when pages stuck together with wax in the place of letters — beware not to separate them [Peri Megadim]. And know further Shevut Yaakov's words are specifically regarding wax; but regarding ink that stuck on a letter — he also agrees there is erasing, for here the ink is not like cover from above, for behold the letter was nullified initially thereby, as above in siman 332 seif 17; and through erasing the ink a letter is newly formed; and even though it is engraved — no distinction for Shabbat, as Rosh and Rashba wrote end of chapter HaBoneh; and Rashba's view who holds engraved applies also to Shabbat is solitary, as Chiddushei R' Akiva Eiger wrote on siman 332 — see there [but accordingly his liability would be for writing as there in the Gemara regarding removing the roof of a chet and making two zayins; and from Bach it appears somewhat his liability is for erasing, as in Eliyah Rabbah and Eshel Avraham — requires study]. And behold according to what Chiddushei R' Akiva Eiger clarified on siman 332 that glue's law is like ink — see there; if so when pages stuck together with glue in the place of letters — d'oraisa prohibition for all not to separate them; and even if you strain and say regarding letters the glue peels from them also for Gra like wax — at minimum for Bach and supporters there is d'oraisa prohibition:`,
  "4:_": `In liquids on the table — see in Mishna Berurah where we copied laws of writing and erasing; its essence is from Rambam chapter 1 and the Gemara there; and it is further proven there in the Gemara that if he wrote in teria water [Rashi explained in one language it is a type of fruit, and in second language rain water] or in dust [meaning outside water, as concluded in Gittin 19] or in soot [see Rashi and Tosafos there; and see in Even HaEzer siman 125 that for Rambam he would be liable in all such cases] — liable; and in Tosefta it further states if he wrote on walnut shells, pomegranate shells [meaning he wrote in their juice that dyes — as explained in Sefer Chasidei David] — all these are called writing in something enduring and he is liable to a chatat; and Rambam included all this in what he wrote "and the like"; and see Rashba Shabbos 115b s.v. had amrinan that regarding Shabbat liability we do not require writing on something that endures forever, only that endure somewhat until people's manner is to write in them things not made to endure forever but for a time, such as memorandum books and the like — regarding Shabbat it is melacha thought and we do not require it endure forever like ink; and similarly implied is Tosafos s.v. aval hacha — see there; and accordingly it appears simple that one who writes on a lead tablet as is customary today among merchants to write their accounts as a reminder — liable, and this is not comparable to writing in dust, for it is clear there in Gittin that it is not called writing d'oraisa, for we see that on a lead tablet its mark is recognizable and endures; and all the more writing with charcoal mixed in water — the Gemara implies liable to a chatat; and all the more according to Mechaber there in Even HaEzer siman 125 who ruled anonymously like Rambam that in dust and soot in all such cases valid — if so in our matter certainly liable in all cases. And regarding if he wrote in non-enduring matter — forbidden — it further states there in the mishnah if he wrote in road dust and scribe's dust — see there in Rashi and Rambam took part of them; and the same for all these whose mark does not endure; and likewise what we require he write on something enduring — Tosefta states if he wrote on olive, carob, and gourd leaves, or wrote in congealed blood and milk [meaning by scratching, for it does not say on congealed blood, as it concludes on olive leaf — see there] — liable; and on lettuce, leek, onion leaves and vegetables — exempt, for it is not permanent; and Rashba brought this Tosefta for halacha — see there; and Rambam took what occurred and included the rest in his words in what he wrote he writes enduring matter on enduring matter; and what he did not mention milk and blood — a great expansion — requires study. And behold I saw an astonishing matter in Peri Megadim who wanted to distinguish between equals between Shabbat and gittin against Rashba from what Rambam and Yerushalmi ruled that on vegetable leaves exempt, and in Gittin 17a it states they write on an olive leaf and it was hidden from him this Tosefta that on Shabbat too liable on olive leaf; and know further that if he wrote letters on foods — likewise liable, for it is called on something enduring, as proven in Gittin 19a — according to Rabbanan if he wrote on them a get it is valid, for it is called something enduring; only according to R' Yehuda HaGelili they limited regarding get from scripture — see there in the Gemara his reason they require similar to a book; and likewise explicit in Mordechai chapter Kol Gadol that erasing applies and automatically writing applies also. And regarding writing one letter see Mishna Berurah — specifically if he completed the book; and it implies if he completed only a word with this he is not liable; and Tosefta states the reverse; and see Rashba. And know that even though Rashi s.v. vehishlimah lesfar explained last letter of one of the twenty-four books — it appears not precise, for the same if some letter was missing in the middle of the book — through this it is not a complete book and he fixed it — liable, for through this the book was completed; and likewise proven in Rashi s.v. rava amar and Ri'az brought in Shiltei HaGibborim — see there. And regarding correcting one letter we copied Rambam's words; and in the Gemara there is further an establishment such as he took the roof of a dalet and made a resh; and this is brought in Rosh and Rabbeinu Yerucham; and what Rambam did not copy — because Rava deals such as through this the book was completely corrected with no further error except this — therefore liable, for it is like completing the book, as Rashi explained; and if so this is included in what he already copied regarding completing the book. And regarding writing equal letters we copied Rambam's language such as a word is formed thereby; and if they were equal and no word formed — such as alef-alef, bet-bet and the like — from Rambam's language it appears exempt, as Rambam wrote; and his view is from all the conditions of the baraita it appears he holds alef-alef of "I girded you" does not obligate; and how does the tanna of the mishnah argue on all these — therefore what the mishnah said "whether from one name" means specifically such as gag-tav and the like; and he ruled like the anonymous mishnah and like R' Gamliel who is R' Yehuda's teacher and not like R' Yehuda [and also possible he holds like R' Yaakov's establishment that the mishnah is R' Yosi who obligates because of marking and therefore liable in all cases; and he rules like all baraita conditions that argue on this]; but Rashi's, Ri'az's, and Ra'avad's view — even on alef and alef liable; and likewise implied from Rif and Rosh who copied the mishnah that said whether from one name whether from two names anonymously and did not copy R' Yehuda's baraita from R' Gamliel — see there in the Gemara it appears they hold also on alef-alef liable. One who examines Rashi's view throughout the sugya will see he holds the baraita tanna'im do not argue on the mishnah at all, and all hold if he intended from the start to write two alefs and the like — liable; and what they said exempt — specifically when he intended from the start to write one large word with this — they hold that even though the Torah revealed letter by letter that even if he wrote part of it he is liable — nevertheless we require that in that part there be some word as he originally intended; therefore exempt on alef-alef of "I girded you," for there is no word at all; and R' Shimon who exempts until he writes the whole name — also deals thus, as proven from Rashi s.v. et kulo; but if from the start he intended to write only two letters — for all liable, even alef-alef, even for R' Shimon. And regarding two symbols we copied from Rambam that liable; and at first glance difficult, for Yerushalmi states on this: who is the tanna of two symbols? R' Yosi [meaning he obligates even in two marks, therefore liable even without letters only in symbols alone] — implies for Rabbanan exempt; and if so Rambam who ruled not like R' Yosi — since he ruled writing with left exempt — automatically exempt with two symbols; and one can say Rambam holds the Bavli argues on this and proof from what they challenge on Shabbat 103 on R' Yaakov's answer "but from the end R' Yosi the beginning is not R' Yosi" — implies even for Abbaye it works; or that the Gemara holds even for Rabbanan liable, for symbols are like letters. And regarding read together — question if he wrote one letter and afterward wrote another above it — whether this is called two letters after they are not read together; and do not bring proof from one in land and one on beam exempt — because they were not adjacent but one above the other; one can say the mishnah uses "not this and not this" language — see there in the mishnah. And see Yerushalmi that commentator explained it is included in writing atop writing — meaning one letter written above another and exempt because not read together; and their explanation in Yerushalmi is not forced, as I wrote in my chiddushim; and also our Gemara does not hold this explanation, for otherwise whence that it is not like R' Yehuda. And regarding marking lines we copied Rambam's language; its source is Yerushalmi chapter Kol Gadol: the first who outlines a form is liable for writing and the second for dyeing; explanation: the first marked the form on hide and the like and the second passed dye on it — therefore the first liable only for writing for the marked image he made, and the second who passed dye on the marked place is only dyeing; and Rambam's intent is also that he made the mark of the image as painters mark. And behold Rambam did not explain explicitly whether we require two forms specifically like in letters or here liable even in one, for it is considered independently; and from this Yerushalmi it is clear even one form liable; and see in Eshel Avraham who challenged Rambam's words here against what he ruled in chapter 10 and we copied in Mishna Berurah that one who outlines in a formed utensil — his liability is striking the final hammer-blow; and with our words it is somewhat resolved, for in chapter 10 it does not deal with mere marking as in our matter, for here the complete melacha for adornment is not yet done but he made the form at once — therefore marking does not apply, for it is a different matter; unlike in our matter it deals that he made only the mark of the form initially so the dyer knows thereby afterward what to dye — similar to writing letters that indicate something; and that Rambam does not obligate in a utensil for dyeing — perhaps it does not deal when he outlined in dye but engraved a form in a utensil as done on silver utensils — therefore he also did not mention in our Gemara daf 76b dyeing, only striking the final hammer-blow; and in what we wrote Peri Megadim's challenge in s.k. 6 is removed. And incidentally since we deal with writing laws I saw fit to note one matter that troubled me: Rashi on the mishnah of writing on two pages of a ledger wrote they coat with wax — implies wax is called something enduring; and likewise reasonable it is not inferior to congealed milk that Tosefta states liable for writing on it; and below in siman 645 seif 7 it states on wax tablets it is non-enduring; and Hagahot Maimoniyot wrote there that even on Shabbat exempt. And regarding erasing we copied its essence from Rambam's language; and what he wrote in order to write in the place of erasure — it appears he learned thus from what the Gemara said and Rambam ruled thus that demolishing in order to build in its place is demolishing, etc. — see there daf 31b. Peri Megadim wrote: that we require erasing in order to write means he intends it be fit whenever he wishes to write two letters on it, not that he intend to write on Shabbat; and obvious:`,
  "5:א": `Permitted, etc. — behold Bach and Taz challenged Shulchan Aruch's words from the mishnah in chapter HaBoneh that marking is like writing, for so they mark on Mishkan boards to know which is its mate; and if he marked two marks on something enduring — liable to a chatat; if so on non-enduring matter must be forbidden rabbinically, as in the mishnah there regarding writing on non-enduring matter, as above; and therefore Bach ruled against Tur and Mechaber and wrote forbidden to mark on a fingernail even one mark; and Magen Avraham and Taz expanded on this and reconciled with difficulty — see there. And the truth is as Eshel Avraham sided to say Tur holds the halacha is not like R' Yosi, as Ra'avad wrote; and if so mere scratch-marks are not included in writing at all and forbidden only rabbinically; therefore on non-enduring matter entirely permitted — see there; and it is clear this is also Rif's, Rosh's, Rambam's, and Rabbeinu Yerucham's view, not like R' Yosi, for behold all ruled writing with left exempt unless proficient with both hands — this is not like R' Yosi, as stated in the Gemara; and this is also Shulchan Aruch's reason; yet Darkei Moshe brought in name of Or Zarua to forbid, concerned for R' Yosi's view that marking is liable like writing; and if so on non-enduring matter forbidden rabbinically; and Kenesset HaGedolah wrote proper for God-fearing to be stringent; and likewise in Birkei Yosef; and see what I further wrote below on Shulchan Aruch's reason [gloss: incidentally I write an astonishing matter I saw in Maggid Mishnah chapter 1 of Hilchot Shabbat law 17 s.v. marking, etc., on what Rambam wrote there law 17: marking is a toldah of writing — how? The first marks lines and forms on a wall with scarlet and the like, etc.; and Maggid Mishnah wrote: marking is a toldah of writing. Mishnah in HaBoneh: R' Yosi said they did not obligate two letters except because of marking, for so they write on Mishkan boards to know which is its mate; and Rambam explained in explanation of the mishnah R' Yosi's view was that marking is an av by itself and writing two letters alone is because of marking, for so they did in the Mishkan; and the halacha is not like him but a toldah of writing — end Maggid Mishnah; and I did not understand his explanation, for behold writing two letters is actual writing and not a toldah, as in the mishnah daf 73; and if to say his intent regarding mere scratch-marks that it is a toldah of writing and what Rambam wrote there that halacha is not like R' Yosi — meaning it is not an av by itself — also not, for Rambam there does not deal with scratch-marks at all but whether writing two letters' liability is for writing or marking — see there; and what is there in the Gemara 72b according to R' Yosi if he scratched two marks liable — automatically we derive this, since R' Yosi holds two letters are not yet included in writing but only because of marking liable, for it is a mere sign — if so even two scratches are included; and automatically for Rambam who explicitly ruled in explanation of the mishnah halacha is not like R' Yosi but two letters' liability is for writing — we have no proof for marking included in melacha, for on Mishkan boards they wrote letters, as implied in mishnah explanation there and likewise in R' Yosi's baraita for his reason he innovated this law that scratching is included in melacha, concluding therefore if he scratched one mark, etc. — see there implies for the anonymous tanna exempt in scratching; and likewise implied there in the Gemara therefore exempt for the anonymous tanna in one proficient with left; and also all Maggid Mishnah's proofs he brought R' Yosi's words — here is astonishing, for this marking Rambam mentioned is a different matter, for behold he concludes how one marks lines, etc. [and his words are explicit in Yerushalmi chapter Kol Gadol law 2 — see there] and he did not mention scratch-marks at all — implies he holds this is not included in writing at all; and likewise from what he ruled writing with left exempt, as above; and likewise from what he ruled in law 10 regarding writing two letters — specifically when there is some word such as tav-tav, chet-chet liable, but alef-alef exempt, as Maggid Mishnah wrote there; and if we say even two marks liable — this is not inferior to two marks; and likewise all baraita conditions that hold alef-alef of "I girded you" does not obligate — they also hold mere marks exempt]:`,
  "5:ב": `To mark, etc. — see in Peri Megadim who wrote: nevertheless when he marks on a fingernail a mark on an error in a book — it appears forbidden, for correcting fixes thereby and I am concerned for chatat — see Shabbos 104b; and specifically to mark as a reminder and the like one can say permitted — end; and from other Acharonim' words it does not appear so; and the law is with them that there it deals correcting the letter through his correction, but here he only makes a line as a reminder and the fixing will be done after Shabbat; and perhaps Peri Megadim's intent is when he erases the letter through marking with his nail — strained, for certainly Shulchan Aruch does not deal thus and what does it teach us:`,
  "5:ג": `As one marks — see in Mishna Berurah what he wrote that to make like a letter forbidden — from Levush and Acharonim. And behold from Gra's words in seif 4 where he proved the source for the gloss there permitting to mark in the air like letters [even many] — from this seif it appears he explains Shulchan Aruch's words even like letters; and accordingly I did not know any reason for Shulchan Aruch permitting l'chatchila because it is non-enduring matter — at minimum rabbinically should be forbidden; and perhaps this is Gra's reason that he did not copy any source for Shulchan Aruch's words in this seif; nevertheless question why he did not challenge this and was silent; and perhaps the reason is he holds Shulchan Aruch holds marking is not considered writing at all in all cases; and proof from Yerushalmi chapter 23: one who marks on light the form of writing exempt; and likewise Rambam ruled — see there Maggid Mishnah whose source is this Yerushalmi; and therefore on non-enduring matter permitted l'chatchila; and one can say Yerushalmi deals marking on a fingernail because it is non-enduring; and therefore exempt; and it is known exempt matters of Shabbat exempt but forbidden is stated anonymously; and furthermore if not they should have distinguished in marking itself between in a utensil and on a fingernail; and why distinguish there between tearing on hide like the form of writing and marking on hide like the form of writing; however in Or Zarua the Yerushalmi reads the reverse: one who marks on light the form of writing liable; and likewise in our Tosefta; and Gra did not emend this at all — implies he holds this is primary; and accordingly to mark on a fingernail, which is non-enduring matter — at minimum forbidden l'chatchila, as the aforementioned Acharonim; and according to our words this entire law depends on Tosefta's version whether like Rambam or like Or Zarua; and likewise Maggid Mishnah wrote there are variant readings in Tosefta; and Gra did well not to write on this neither source but critique; and still requires study in all this:`,
  "13:א": `One does not break, etc. — this seif and the adjacent one are from Rambam's language; his words in this seif derive from what he said in Beitzah daf 32: the reason is he makes it a utensil; and at first glance according to what is proven in the adjacent seif that tearing in order to fix is liable for tearing — why not say here on account of tearing; and furthermore from Rambam's language in chapter 23 it appears it is rabbinic, for he included it there with several matters known to be rabbinic; and if there were tearing concern it would be d'oraisa prohibition; and in truth Peri Megadim already challenged this and it remains in difficulty; and what one of the Acharonim wanted to derive that tearing applies only in something sewn and woven joined from many bodies as in curtains, but not in something of one body like hide and paper — this is not, for Yerushalmi explicitly deals tearing also in hide; and Neshmat Adam wanted to say thus and rejected it from this Yerushalmi. And were I not afraid I would say a novel matter that tearing in order to fix applies only when he tears something in the middle and needs fixing of both parts, as in the Mishkan where a curtain had a tear they tore and sewed it [Shabbos 74]; and likewise what is said there Shabbos 104 regarding a servant like a pouch — the benefit is for the whole garment; and when he tears in anger Rambam obligates because through this he soothes himself — like fixing the whole body of the garment; unlike when he tears something from the side of the garment to fix the garment that was long or defective at its edge — the torn piece is not fixed at all — tearing in order to fix does not apply, only another name fixing a utensil by fixing the garment [and if particular about measure it appears there is also mechatech concern; see above siman 322 what is written on mechatech]; and the matter depends on the fixing whether complete fixing — liable for striking the final hammer-blow completing the utensil or garment; and if not complete fixing — rabbinic; therefore in our matter where he tears some piece of paper from a whole page in order to use it for something and in the page from which paper was torn he does not fix it at all and possibly spoils it — perhaps tearing in order to fix does not apply and depends only on fixing done to the torn piece of paper; and Rambam holds what the Gemara said there he makes it a utensil means it appears like fixing a utensil that is not a complete utensil thereby; therefore Rambam included this law in every matter rabbinically; and accordingly if he tears the paper into many pieces and needs each — liable, for he tears in order to fix; and he supported from what is said in Moed Katan 22b on the verse "and he strengthened in his garments and tore them into two tears" — implies plain tearing is into two and not tearing some piece from the side and discarding it while the garment remains in place [and some support for this from Yerushalmi chapter Kol Gadol where it was difficult: is tearing cutting? and it says tearing in the middle, cutting from the side; and according to our words likewise tearing is only in the middle in order that both sides be fixed through the tearing as in the Mishkan, as above; but cutting applies even from the side when he cuts at the edge of the hide to even it, as Yerushalmi says there a woman weaving who cuts the threads — there is mechatech concern; but one can reject Yerushalmi took customary language; and its intent in saying tearing in the middle means at its edge sometimes impossible except by cutting, for nothing to grasp and tear; and all that we needed to reconcile the Gemara's language that it says because he makes it a utensil and not tearing is for Rambam's view that opening the neck of a garment is also forbidden on account of tearing; but according to Rashi there who also [because he makes it a utensil — see there daf 48 and his reason as we wrote in Biur Halacha below] holds every matter whose fixing is recognizable immediately at the time of tearing itself — tearing name does not apply at all since he makes a utensil in his tearing — therefore the Gemara could not say the reason tearing; and it can be according to Rashi there is d'oraisa prohibition of striking the final hammer-blow as there daf 48 where Rashi explicitly wrote his liability is striking the final hammer-blow; and the same here; however requires study somewhat, for from Rambam's language it does not appear he holds thus, for he wrote in law 10 one who tears to destroy exempt because he spoils — implies specifically to destroy, but here where there is fixing on some side liable; and one can reject this piece he tears he discards — therefore included in destroying; and see Rambam chapter 1 law 17 regarding one concerned for the candle and extinguishes the wick — liable even though extinguishing is included in demolishing and spoiling as implied in the Gemara 41b — nevertheless liable because he fixes the candle thereby; and at first glance contrary to our words; and even if we say Rambam holds like our rabbis' view brought there in Rashi and calls all this for him demolishing in order to build in its place, as we clarified above in Biur Halacha — at minimum for Rashi proven explicitly demolishing in order to build not in its place is called spoiling here and fixing elsewhere and nevertheless liable according to the anonymous tana; but already we clarified for Rashi from one reason it is not included in tearing — requires study in all this]:`,
  "13:ב": `Paper — see in Mishna Berurah what we wrote regarding opening a letter; and I will explain somewhat. Perach in Yoreh De'ah 118 wrote an Israelite who opens it — chatat liability for all, for tearing paper is spoiling in order to fix and its fixing is what is needed to open it; and likewise Sefer HaZichronot — spoiling in order to fix; and in responsum Chacham Tzvi siman 39 he disagrees and permitted through a non-Jew and did not explain his reason; and it appears he holds melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah, for he does not fix the paper itself immediately; and Tosafos daf 94 wrote therefore he tears in anger to frighten members of his household exempt according to R' Shimon because there is no fixing to the torn thing itself; but accordingly for Rambam who rules melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah liable — d'oraisa prohibition here and forbidden through a non-Jew; and in Neshmat Adam general rule 29 I saw he wrote for all exempt; and his intent is different that case of tearing for a dead person and in anger where the tearing itself is the fixing [for through tearing he soothes anger or frightens household members] unlike here where for the paper it is pure spoiling and fixing is from another side; and in truth neither Chacham Tzvi's nor Neshmat Adam's view is clear here, for it is known the essential fixing of a letter is to read it and its fixing is that he can read it; and if so when he tears in order to read it — melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah; however where the letter is enclosed within another paper as is customary — certainly one who tears the upper paper is included in spoiling and exempt even for R' Yehuda, as we say Shabbos 93b spoiling exempt even for R' Yehuda who holds melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah liable; and therefore permitted through a non-Jew where needed, for it is shevut di-shevut; but according to what we brought above that according to Rashi where it is demolishing in order to build not in its place — spoiling here and fixing elsewhere — liable for R' Yehuda; if so here in all cases d'oraisa melacha for R' Yehuda, included in tearing in order to fix; and even through a non-Jew beware; and likewise Magen Avraham in siman 307 s.k. 20 sided to forbid like Sefer HaZichronot; nevertheless it appears for great need one may be lenient to open through a non-Jew, especially if the letter is placed within another paper; and l'chatchila better to do as I wrote in Mishna Berurah in name of Agudah:`,
  "14:א": `This is a toldah of sewing — for sewing's matter is taking two separate things and joining them as one; and likewise through adhesion; and it appears the same when he glues wood to wood with glue as is customary — likewise sewing on it, for we find in the Gemara daf 74b that in wood too sewing applies, as stated there: this one who makes a basket — if he sewed its mouth, etc. — see there in Rashi:`,
  "14:ב": `And liable — see in Mishna Berurah what we wrote regarding measure. And behold even though regarding grinding Rambam in chapter 8 law 15 holds one who saws wood to enjoy the sawdust — even though toldah of grinding and grinding's measure is like a dried fig — nevertheless here liability is for any amount, for there is no necessity from the av to the toldah regarding measure there, for sawdust's measure is considered for any amount; and likewise scraping metal whose metal is considered for any amount — see there in Rambam and Maggid Mishnah; unlike regarding gluing hide which is like sewing — we find no distinction in sewing between hide garment and other garment; and in all we require measure for liability; and the same regarding gluing. And regarding paper it appears from Shulchan Aruch's language likewise one law for them; but this requires study regarding separating, for his liability is for tearing; and even though not in order to sew, since his intent is for some need and not to spoil — considered like in order to sew, as below; or tearing in anger [according to Rambam who obligates] — whether measure of two stitches applies or perhaps when we say in tearing this measure — specifically where liability is because intent was in order to sew; therefore in less than this measure not considered; unlike where liability is because intent is for some other fixing, such as in our matter where paper and hide must be separated, or tearing in anger to settle his mind as Rambam wrote, or any tearing in order to fix — whenever there is the fixing he intends he is liable and does not depend on this measure. And know in tearing for a dead person we say in the Gemara liable because there is mitzva fixing through the tearing — if so when there is tefach measure, for less than this not measure of tearing even for other mourners as written in Moed Katan and he did not fix at all — behold it does not depend on two stitches' measure; if so possible the same in every matter according to its fixing, whether to lenient or stringent [and our words are according to Tosafos there 105b s.v. ha r' y — see there; but according to Rambam there chapter 10 law 10 and on this mishnah his reason is his heat and pain are soothed through the tear — not clear there too if specifically tefach]; and behold what is in the mishnah spoiling in order to fix — its measure is like the fixing, meaning in a matter that has known fixing measure like erasing in order to write and tearing in order to sew and demolishing in order to build — each according to its measure, as in Rambam chapter 1 law 18 — see there; and if so likewise tearing in order to fix some fixing — measure depends on what is needed and does not depend on two stitches — requires study:`,
  "14:ג": `Only — all this is Rambam's language chapter 10 of Hilchot Shabbat law 10; and his view is clearly implied that what the Gemara requires tearing in order to sew is because otherwise it would be spoiling; but if he intends for some fixing benefit, even intending not in order to sew — liable; and with this it works that Rambam included opening the neck of a garment on Shabbat also in tearing laws for liability, even though certainly not in order to sew, since he fixes the garment through his tearing; and wonder at Beit Yosef who wrote in siman 317 on the obvious matter that opening the neck of a garment for this reason is not included in tearing; and he himself ruled in siman 340 like Rambam that all tearing in order to fix is liable; and in truth proven explicitly from the Gemara (Shabbos 105b) regarding tearing in anger and over a dead person lying on him [those mentioned in the parashah: his father and mother, his son, etc., of whom some may not be sewn afterward] that even not in order to sew liable whenever there is some fixing in the tearing. And afterward I found this proof brought in Gra's gloss and also all the above matters are essentially explained in Neshmat Adam general rule 29 in laws of Shabbat — see there; and we will speak on this below; and Peri Megadim hinted to this [but what Gra wrote from Tosafos 93b s.v. vetzarikh it appears we require specifically in order to sew — can be rejected that Tosafos' language is loose and their intent is when he does not need the wood there is no harvesting concern for liability, for he makes no fixing in the cut thing itself; and similar to tearing where we require in order to sew or fix — it is similar to sewing; otherwise no liability, for he spoils and it is not included in melacha; the same here; and know Rambam certainly holds we do not require specifically in order to sew, and he also holds melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah liable, and nevertheless rules in pruning that specifically when he needs the wood then liable for harvesting; and therefore what Tosafos wrote in other houses — lest he forget, since there is no opposing concern — meaning no agitation in other houses and certainly they do not permit muktzeh — can be said also according to Tosafos; and afterward they strengthened their view much from that cut branches have no harvesting at all, and likewise there is no threshing at all even to forbid l'chatchila; and also daf 94a Tosafos wrote only in spoilings such as extinguishing and demolishing it is reasonable to require in order to fix like in the Mishkan, etc. — see there and they did not mention in order to sew at all; and therefore as we wrote on account of fix is like in order to sew; and likewise below daf 73 Rashi wrote tearing not in order to sew was not in the Mishkan — also deals when there was no intent to fix, for this is like in order to sew; we do not require specifically as in the Mishkan itself; see daf 75 end of their words; and compelling proof for all our words from tearing over a dead person or in anger; and as above]. And I saw in Neshmat Adam he wrote the law of separating papers and likewise all tearing in order to fix — specifically if we hold melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah liable; but if we hold exempt — requires specifically tearing in order to sew literally; and because of this he wondered at Shulchan Aruch who brought Rambam's law anonymously, for behold in siman 316 and siman 334 he sided more with those who exempt; and his words are not clear to me at all, for needless to say according to Tosafos there regarding tearing over his dead that where there is mitzva fixing it is called melacha needed for its purpose since he needs it — the same in all tearing in order to fix; and even according to Rashi and supporters who hold even tearing over his dead exempt because melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah — meaning they hold need of mitzva is not considered fixing enough to be called melacha needed for its purpose; but in all fixing matters needed in themselves, such as in separating papers where he needs paper thin — for all melacha needed for its purpose like tearing in order to sew; and what we exempt there in the Gemara regarding tearing in anger for one who exempts melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah — because he fixes nothing in the garment body through his tearing, even when he frightens household members, as Tosafos wrote daf 94; and also Rashi's view is proven thus, for he wrote there tearing over his dead exempt because melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah — like taking out the dead for burial [meaning willingly it did not come to him and he did not need this whole matter of tearing in anger and over his dead]; and he did not write plainly the reason because it is not in order to sew; rather certainly even not in order to sew liable even according to Rashi where the fixing itself he wants the tearing for. And likewise implied from Me'or's words in chapter R' Eliezer Omer at the end of his words who wrote what R' Shimon exempts even tearing to frighten household members — because the need is not in the melacha itself — see there implies if he needed the tearing itself, even not in order to sew liable; and likewise Rabbeinu Yerucham part 12 ruled explicitly like R' Shimon and nevertheless part 14 one who tears over a dead person whom he is obligated to mourn — liable; and like Tosafos' view behold tearing in order to fix liable; and likewise implied in Meiri chapter HaOreg explicitly tearing in order to fix even not in order to sew liable — see there. The conclusion of our words: whether according to Rambam or Rashi and Tosafos and all the aforementioned poskim — tearing in order to fix liable, like in order to sew; and even according to R' Shimon who exempts in melacha she'einah tzerichah legufah liable here; except according to Tosafos we require for R' Shimon fixing in the body of the torn thing itself — see there daf 94a; and Ritva's view brought in many places chapter 10 of Hilchot Shabbat who holds we require tearing in order to sew specifically is solitary; and also the sugya of HaOreg is against him, as Gra challenged; and also Beit Yosef, though he too wanted to say thus in siman 317 — behold Shulchan Aruch here returned and ruled anonymously tearing in order to fix liable even not in order to sew; and likewise proven in Perach Yoreh De'ah siman 118 tearing in order to fix liable for all; and in vain one challenged him in Neshmat Adam general rule 29 on this. And nothing remains except to explain Rashi's view daf 48 who wrote opening the neck of a garment on Shabbat — his liability is striking the final hammer-blow because he makes it a utensil; and why not write on account of tearing in order to fix; and it appears his view is tearing in order to fix applies only when he spoils at the time of the action but intends for some fixing similar to the Mishkan where a curtain had a tear they tore and sewed — the tearing spoils the curtain more, only thereby they can afterward sew it; and similarly what is said there Shabbos 104 regarding servant like pouch — benefit for whole garment; and when he tears something from the side of the garment intending to fix the garment that was long or defective at its edge — the torn piece is not fixed — tearing in order to fix does not apply; unlike opening the neck of a garment on Shabbat where he makes a utensil through the opening itself — tearing name does not apply at all and liable on another name, striking the final hammer-blow, which applies in every melacha completion; and likewise proven there in the Gemara in a baraita he makes it a utensil in one category with not placing stuffing into a cushion, and as striking the final hammer-blow applies here; and also Rambam was concerned with this except he follows his view in chapter 22 law 23 — see there; this is my humble opinion on Rashi's reason and other poskim who wrote on account of striking the final hammer-blow; and Shulchan Aruch ruled anonymously in siman 317 seif 3; and there is no contradiction with this seif; and see Magen Avraham siman 317 s.k. 10 who rejected Eshel Avraham's words who wrote opening the neck of a garment — his liability is tearing; and according to what we clarified the law with Magen Avraham regarding opening the neck of a garment, but not in all tearing matters, as Mechaber ruled anonymously here that in all tearing in order to fix liable:`,
  "14:ד": `And liable — see in Mishna Berurah what is written regarding tearing new pages, etc.; and see in Peri Megadim whose words imply on account of tearing in order to fix; and in my view this is unnecessary, for such melacha is included in striking the final hammer-blow, for it is known bookbinders' manner to review and cut at the end all pages joined together; and see what is written above in Rashi's view that where fixing is done immediately at the time of the action — not included in tearing, only fixing a utensil which is striking the final hammer-blow, as Rashi explained daf 48 — see there; and certainly in our matter likewise; and likewise in Sefer Tiferet Yisrael there is fixing a utensil concern; and perhaps Rambam also concedes in our matter there is melacha completion concern, as written at the beginning; and in opening the neck of a garment he holds it is not yet complete melacha:`,
};
